# Asker two-host deploy — PC (RTX 3090 + 128GB) env template.
#
# On the PC: copy this to deploy/compose/.env (gitignored, auto-loaded by
# compose), then run `make pc-up`. If you already have a deploy/compose/.env
# (e.g. real OAuth creds), MERGE these lines into it rather than overwriting.
# See docs/two-host-deploy.md.

# === REQUIRED ================================================================
# How the Mac (browser + workers) reaches THIS PC. Use the PC's STATIC LAN IP.
# This is also what Redpanda advertises as its Kafka address, so it must be
# routable from the Mac. Do NOT use a *.local (Bonjour/mDNS) name: the Mac's
# workers run inside Docker Desktop's Linux VM, which does not resolve macOS
# mDNS, so a .local value never connects. Example: 192.168.1.50
PC_HOST=192.168.1.50

# Which PC interface to bind the LAN-exposed ports to. Defaults (in compose) to
# 127.0.0.1 = LOOPBACK ONLY: leaving this unset/loopback means nothing is on the
# LAN and the Mac can't connect (fail safe). Set it to the PC's specific LAN IP
# to enable the two-host topology. SECURITY: the exposed ports carry dev creds
# with NO auth — Redpanda :19092 is the whole document corpus, Redis :16379 holds
# per-tenant profiles/cache — so bind to the LAN IP on a TRUSTED WIRED segment,
# never to 0.0.0.0 on a host that also has a WiFi/VPN/public interface.
PC_LAN_IP=192.168.1.50

# === GPU =====================================================================
# TEI CUDA image for your card. 86-1.8 = Ampere/sm86 (RTX 3090, A10).
# Others: 89-1.8 (Ada/RTX 4090), turing-1.8 (T4), hopper-1.8 (H100).
TEI_IMAGE=ghcr.io/huggingface/text-embeddings-inference:86-1.8
# CUDA torch wheel index for the CLIP build. cu126 ships torch==2.12.0 (pinned
# in requirements.txt); cu124 does NOT. CUDA 12.6 runs on the RTX 3090 (Ampere).
CLIP_TORCH_INDEX_URL=https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu126
# CLIP device: auto (cuda if visible), or force cuda|cpu.
CLIP_DEVICE=auto

# TEI CPU/threads. The base caps these to 4 to stop the EMULATED amd64 image
# from thrashing a small Apple-Silicon dev VM — pointless on the native-x86 GPU
# host, where CPU-side tokenization feeds the 3090. Raise so tokenization isn't
# the bottleneck when draining an indexing backlog. (Base wires cpus + RAYON +
# OMP all to TEI_CPUS; the pc overlay raises tokenization-workers/batch too.)
TEI_CPUS=16
TEI_TOKENIZATION_WORKERS=8
TEI_MAX_BATCH_TOKENS=16384
TEI_MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS=64

# === Memory (128GB host: give the engine real room) ==========================
# Vespa query-container heap (substituted into services.xml by vespa/deploy.sh).
VESPA_CONTAINER_JVM=-Xms2g -Xmx8g
# Vespa config-server heap.
VESPA_CONFIGSERVER_JVMARGS=-Xms512m -Xmx2g
# Redpanda allocation (the async backbone; base dev value is 512M / 1 core).
REDPANDA_SMP=4
REDPANDA_MEMORY=8G

# === Models (MUST match across PC TEI/CLIP, the Mac workers, and Vespa) =======
# bge-m3 => 1024. Keep in sync with the Mac's .env and the deployed Vespa schema.
EMBEDDING_DIM=1024
CLIP_DIM=512
